# Inbound TCP — io.net.listen. Where io.net's httpGet/httpPost are OUTBOUND
# only (this instance calling out, bounded by ioHTTPClient's 5s timeout),
# io.net.listen is the other direction: a daemon body binds a real listening
# socket and accepts real inbound connections, one at a time, forever. It is
# a strictly bigger trust boundary than making an outbound call (anyone who
# can reach this port can open a connection), hence the separate, more
# specific capability name — a daemon opts into it by name, never by
# accident via a plain `uses io.net`.
#
# accept()/readBytes() below block indefinitely — there is no timeout,
# because there cannot be one: an accept loop's whole job is to wait,
# possibly forever, for the next client. That is exactly why listen/accept/
# readBytes/writeBytes/closeConn are restricted to a daemon body and refused
# everywhere else (internal/ir/build.go's checkDaemonOnlyBuiltins) — a daemon
# is the one place in this runtime that runs detached, under no request's
# store lock, for as long as it likes (runtime/daemon.go's doc). Reached via
# an ordinary proc (`do`/`spawn`+`join` from an action), the exact same
# accept() call would hold the durable-store lock hostage for however long
# it takes a client to connect — possibly the rest of the process's life.
#
# Connections are handled ONE AT A TIME, sequentially, by design: structured
# concurrency here (spawn/join) requires a spawned handle be joined before
# the enclosing statement LIST ends (see ast.Spawn's doc) — a handle can
# never outlive the block it was spawned in, and can never be joined in a
# later iteration of an enclosing loop. Handling connections concurrently
# would need a spawn whose join happens on some LATER trip through this
# loop's body, which that rule deliberately forbids. Rather than work around
# it, this accepts the honest, narrower shape: one connection fully served
# (read/echo until EOF) before the next is accepted. A concurrent version is
# real future work (it needs either relaxing the join-scope rule or a
# worker-pool-style primitive that doesn't exist yet), not something to fake
# here.
#
# Verify with a real external client while the server's own HTTP API is
# being hit concurrently, proving the accept loop neither blocks nor is
# blocked by ordinary request handling:
#
#   facet run examples/echo_daemon.fct     # HTTP on :7373, echo on :7000
#   printf 'hello world' | nc -q1 localhost 7000     # -> echoes "hello world"
#   curl -s -X POST localhost:7373/api/bump/1        # ordinary HTTP, unaffected
#   curl -s localhost:7373/api/EchoStats             # connections/bytesEchoed climb

app EchoDaemon:
    entity EchoStats:
        id: int
        connections: int
        bytesEchoed: int

    entity Widget:
        id: int
        count: int

    action seedStats:
        add EchoStats { connections: 0, bytesEchoed: 0 }

    action seedWidget:
        add Widget { count: 0 }

    action recordConnection:
        set EchoStats(1).connections = EchoStats(1).connections + 1

    action recordBytes(n: int):
        set EchoStats(1).bytesEchoed = EchoStats(1).bytesEchoed + n

    # Ordinary request-handling work, hit over plain HTTP below — the thing
    # the echo daemon's blocking accept()/readBytes() must never stall.
    action bump(id: int):
        set Widget(id).count = Widget(id).count + 1

    job seedStatsJob on start -> seedStats
    job seedWidgetJob on start -> seedWidget

    daemon Echo uses io.net.listen:
        let l = listen(7000)
        loop true:
            let c = accept(l)
            act recordConnection()
            loop true:
                let data = readBytes(c, 4096)
                if len(data) == 0:
                    break
                writeBytes(c, data)
                act recordBytes(len(data))
            closeConn(c)

    view Main at "/":
        box:
            text "echo daemon: {EchoStats(1).connections} connections, {EchoStats(1).bytesEchoed} bytes echoed"
            text "widget count: {Widget(1).count}"
            button "bump" -> bump(1)
